  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 15.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 492.43pt, "Abstract ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 508.75pt, "Plasmonic nanopores combine nanofluidic confinement with electromagnetic field enhancement, ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 523.27pt, "enabling optical interrogation of single molecules in sub-wavelength volumes. Yet, direct optical readout ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 537.67pt, "within these metallic geometries has remained challenging due to fluorescence quenching near the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 552.19pt, "surface. Here, we implement DNA-PAINT as a molecular reporter of local optical fields inside plasmonic ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 566.71pt, "nanopores. Transient hybridization of fluorescent imager strands at the nanopore tips yields stochastic ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 581.23pt, "emission bursts that map active binding sites with nanometric precision. By varying the fluorophore-metal ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 595.63pt, "distance using DNA spacers of controlled length, we observe a non-monotonic intensity response ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 610.18pt, "consistent with near-field quenching and plasmonic enhancement, identifying an optimal separation of ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 624.7pt, "around 6 nm. Finally, we extend the concept to dual-material Au/Si nanopores, demonstrating lateral ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 639.22pt, "coupling between plasmonic and semiconducting regions. These results establish DNA-PAINT as a ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 653.62pt, "quantitative probe of nanoscale optical environments in hybrid nanopores. ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 676.18pt, "Keywords: plasmonic nanopores; DNA-PAINT; single-molecule fluorescence; near-field enhancement; ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 690.58pt, "fluorescence quenching; DNA nanorulers; dual-material nanopores; hybrid optoelectronic sensors; ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 705.096pt, "molecular biosensing ")
]

